 @friendly-nemesis4754  I agree with you, but this term was not created to fix anything or provide solutions. It is simply the name of a combination of actions, opinions, ideas, and restrictions that were derogatory or even dangerous to women, minorities, etc.

If you got the idea that the discussion prioritizes women, you are right, because the word “toxic masculinity” is useful for women. It is used by women to identify dangerous behavior that projects violence into their future.

If the only use you see is to hurt innocent men, then create another term with the same use without these drawbacks. Then, mentally replace the term “toxic masculinity” with your word if it fulfills its original purpose (a set of attitudes and behaviors stereotypically associated with or expected of men, considered to have a negative impact on men and society as a whole).

 @sabinacristina4125  In the first place, why should any designation of 'Masculinity' be considered antithetical to "minorities"? If you're talking about ethnic groups, I'm pretty sure that about half of the black population (in fact of ALL racial groups) is male, and when you say "etc." I have no idea what you mean, because I see no obvious continuity between the groups on the list that you already named. 

In the second place, I don't have a substitute term, because I don't see the basic linguistic exercise as valid; to what end is it useful to employ any blanket term that targets and alienates a generic demographic when the problem, as you seem to concede, is certain behaviors of certain individuals? Put another way, what do I have to suffer at the hands of people who are demographically distinct from me in order to justify indicting (or using language that is generally perceived as indicting) the demographic itself as representing a risk of some kind of moral/existential malignancy?  

I appreciate your engagement, and await your response.

 @stopprojectingyourbsonme  I'm fine with partaking in any back-and-forth you'd like to have. 

Would you mind explaining in what sense you consider (and have employed it while talking with other women) how the term 'Toxic Masculinity' is relevant in the modern day? Likewise, what do you think are the deeper issues than disparities in strength when it comes to violence between the sexes?

I appreciate your willingness to engage, and look forward to your response.

