 @TookAHikeNowWhat  it actually doesn't make sense. Yes, children take time. But both the biological child and the adopted child require the same amount of attention care and on top of that theres the pregnancy so by math alone the logical deduction should be: pregnancy+care > care. That's why it would also be "interesting" as said by OP, because it would defy common sense.
Also adoption usually happens when a kid is a bit older and so you skip on the most crucial first months that are more intense and time consuming. So now the math is pregnancy+ first couple of critical years of development of the baby+ child care > just child care.
This is why it just doesn't make sense to me and I would love the source to read and understand the argument behind it.

 @YoutubeCategories-id6yu  Yeah, confirmation bias is a real problem even for academics sometimes. While it makes sense to reduce the differences as much as possible between biological and adoptive data sets in order to answer the point of the paper which was that long-term earnings doesn't depend on biology when it comes to raising children, they just did it too much. 
They even included the unweighted data sets briefly, which showed larger differences more consistently, but it was excluded from their conclusions because they thought it's statistically insignificant. 

But the fact that they could only get their data from one country makes it very unreliable for other developed countries.

